with r(n, q, answer, exp, fixed_answer) as (values
(1, $x$The skin is considered the body's:$x$, $x$Largest organ$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Largest organ
The SKIN is the body's LARGEST ORGAN. In an adult it covers about 1.5–2 square metres and weighs about 3.5–4.5 kg (roughly 15% of body weight). It has three layers — epidermis, dermis and subcutaneous tissue (hypodermis) — and important functions: protection against injury, microorganisms and UV light; prevention of water loss; temperature regulation; sensation (touch, pain, temperature); vitamin D production; and excretion of small amounts of waste in sweat. This is why large burns or skin loss (e.g. toxic epidermal necrolysis) are so dangerous — they cause massive fluid loss, heat loss and infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Smallest organ — The skin is the largest organ, not the smallest.
• Only avascular organ — The skin is not avascular — the dermis has a rich blood supply (only the epidermis has no blood vessels).
• Most vascular organ — Although well supplied with blood, the skin is not the "most vascular" organ (e.g. the kidneys and liver receive more blood per gram).

⭐ Key point: Skin assessment (colour, temperature, turgor, integrity) is a key part of ICU care — pressure ulcers can develop within hours in critically ill patients.$x$, null),
(2, $x$G cells, which secrete the hormone gastrin, are located predominantly in the:$x$, $x$Pyloric antrum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pyloric antrum
GASTRIN is a hormone that stimulates the stomach to secrete acid (via parietal cells) and promotes stomach movement. It is produced mainly by G CELLS in the PYLORIC ANTRUM (the lower part of the stomach near the pylorus), with smaller numbers in the duodenum. G cells release gastrin when food (especially protein) enters the stomach and when the stomach is stretched; release is reduced when the stomach becomes very acidic (negative feedback).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fundus — The fundus mainly contains parietal cells (acid, intrinsic factor) and chief cells (pepsinogen).
• Cardia — The cardia contains mainly mucus-secreting glands.
• Duodenum exclusively — The duodenum does contain some G cells, but not "exclusively" — most are in the antrum.

⭐ Key point: A gastrin-secreting tumour (gastrinoma, Zollinger–Ellison syndrome) causes severe, recurrent peptic ulcers from excess acid.$x$, null),
(3, $x$During deep tendon reflex testing, the patellar (knee) reflex primarily assesses the integrity of spinal cord segments$x$, $x$L2-L4 (primarily L3-L4)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: L2-L4 (primarily L3-L4)
The PATELLAR (knee-jerk) REFLEX is tested by tapping the patellar tendon just below the kneecap, which stretches the quadriceps muscle and causes the leg to kick forward. The reflex travels through the FEMORAL NERVE and spinal cord segments L2–L4, mainly L3–L4. An absent or reduced reflex suggests damage to this pathway (e.g. nerve root compression, peripheral neuropathy, Guillain–Barré syndrome, magnesium toxicity); a brisk reflex suggests an upper motor neurone lesion (e.g. stroke, spinal cord injury above this level).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• C5-C6 — C5–C6 are tested by the biceps and brachioradialis reflexes.
• C7-C8 — C7–C8 are tested by the triceps reflex.
• S1-S2 — S1–S2 are tested by the ankle (Achilles) reflex.

⭐ Key point: In women on magnesium sulphate, loss of the patellar reflex is the first sign of magnesium toxicity.$x$, null),
(4, $x$Which lung volume cannot be measured directly by simple spirometry and requires methods like helium dilution or body plethysmography?$x$, $x$Residual volume$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Residual volume
SIMPLE SPIROMETRY measures air moving IN and OUT of the lungs. RESIDUAL VOLUME (RV) is the air that STAYS in the lungs after a maximal breath out — it never leaves, so a spirometer cannot measure it. It must be measured indirectly using HELIUM DILUTION, nitrogen washout, or BODY PLETHYSMOGRAPHY (sitting in a sealed box). Because RV cannot be measured by spirometry, any capacity that includes it (functional residual capacity, total lung capacity) also cannot be measured by spirometry.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tidal volume — Tidal volume (normal breath) can be measured by spirometry.
• Inspiratory reserve volume — Inspiratory reserve volume (extra air breathed in) can be measured.
• Expiratory reserve volume — Expiratory reserve volume (extra air breathed out) can be measured.

⭐ Key point: Residual volume rises in obstructive diseases (COPD, asthma — air trapping) and falls in restrictive diseases (fibrosis, chest wall problems).$x$, null),
(5, $x$Which of the following is NOT a function of the skin?$x$, $x$Production of red blood cells$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Production of red blood cells
The SKIN has many functions: HEAT REGULATION (sweating, changing blood flow to the skin), PROTECTION against microorganisms, injury, chemicals and UV radiation, VITAMIN D SYNTHESIS (UV light converts a cholesterol derivative in the skin into vitamin D3), sensation, and preventing water loss. PRODUCTION OF RED BLOOD CELLS happens in the BONE MARROW (haematopoiesis), not in the skin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Heat regulation — Heat regulation IS a skin function.
• Protection against microorganisms — Protection against microorganisms IS a skin function.
• Vitamin D synthesis — Vitamin D synthesis IS a skin function.

⭐ Key point: When the skin is badly damaged (e.g. burns), patients lose heat and fluid rapidly and are at high risk of infection — keep them warm and use strict asepsis.$x$, null),
(6, $x$Which of the following is NOT one of the classic hormones secreted by the anterior pituitary?$x$, $x$Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)
The ANTERIOR PITUITARY gland makes and releases six main hormones: GROWTH HORMONE (GH), THYROID-STIMULATING HORMONE (TSH), ADRENOCORTICOTROPHIC HORMONE (ACTH), FOLLICLE-STIMULATING HORMONE (FSH), LUTEINISING HORMONE (LH) and PROLACTIN. ANTIDIURETIC HORMONE (ADH, vasopressin) is NOT made by the anterior pituitary — it is made in the HYPOTHALAMUS and STORED and RELEASED from the POSTERIOR PITUITARY (along with oxytocin).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) — FSH IS an anterior pituitary hormone.
• Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) — TSH IS an anterior pituitary hormone.
• Growth hormone (GH) — GH IS an anterior pituitary hormone.

⭐ Key point: Damage to the posterior pituitary (e.g. after pituitary surgery or head injury) can cause diabetes insipidus (lack of ADH) with large volumes of dilute urine.$x$, null),
(7, $x$Titratable acidity refers to renal buffering of secreted hydrogen ions mainly by which urinary buffer?$x$, $x$Phosphate (HPO4^2- converting to H2PO4-)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Phosphate (HPO4^2- converting to H2PO4-)
The kidneys get rid of acid by secreting HYDROGEN IONS (H⁺) into the urine. Free H⁺ in urine would make it too acidic, so it must be BUFFERED. "TITRATABLE ACIDITY" is the H⁺ buffered mainly by PHOSPHATE: H⁺ combines with HPO4²⁻ (monohydrogen phosphate) to form H2PO4⁻ (dihydrogen phosphate), which is excreted. Ammonia (NH3 → NH4⁺) is the other main urinary buffer. For each H⁺ excreted, a new bicarbonate is generated and returned to the blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bicarbonate — Bicarbonate is reabsorbed and generated by the kidney; it is not the urinary buffer for titratable acidity.
• Hemoglobin — Haemoglobin is a buffer in red blood cells, not urine.
• Albumin — Albumin is a plasma protein buffer, not a urinary buffer.

⭐ Key point: In kidney failure, the kidneys cannot excrete acid, causing metabolic acidosis.$x$, null),
(8, $x$Which structure connects the pharynx to the bronchi, serving as the main airway?$x$, $x$Trachea$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Trachea
The TRACHEA (windpipe) is a tube about 10–12 cm long, supported by C-shaped cartilage rings, that runs from the LARYNX (below the pharynx) down into the chest, where it divides (at the carina) into the RIGHT and LEFT MAIN BRONCHI. It is the MAIN AIRWAY carrying air to and from the lungs. Its lining has cilia and mucus that trap and move particles upwards.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oesophagus — The oesophagus carries food from the pharynx to the stomach.
• Aorta — The aorta is the main artery carrying blood from the heart.
• Ureter — The ureter carries urine from the kidney to the bladder.

⭐ Key point: The right main bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left — so ET tubes inserted too far and aspirated material usually enter the right side.$x$, null),
(9, $x$Thick skin differs from thin skin by having an extra layer called the:$x$, $x$Stratum lucidum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stratum lucidum
THICK SKIN (found on the palms of the hands and soles of the feet) has FIVE layers of epidermis. From deep to surface: stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, STRATUM LUCIDUM (a clear, thin layer of dead cells that adds extra protection against friction), and stratum corneum. THIN SKIN (most of the body) has only four layers — it LACKS the stratum lucidum.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stratum basale — Stratum basale is present in both thick and thin skin (it produces new cells).
• Stratum corneum — Stratum corneum is present in both.
• Stratum granulosum — Stratum granulosum is present in both.

⭐ Key point: Memory aid (deep to surface): "Come Let's Get Sun Burned" — Corneum, Lucidum, Granulosum, Spinosum, Basale (read from top down).$x$, null),
(10, $x$Gastrointestinal glandular secretions primarily aid in:$x$, $x$Chemical breakdown of food and enzymatic digestion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chemical breakdown of food and enzymatic digestion
GASTROINTESTINAL GLANDULAR SECRETIONS — saliva, gastric juice (acid, pepsin), pancreatic juice (amylase, lipase, proteases, bicarbonate), bile, and intestinal enzymes — are mainly for the CHEMICAL BREAKDOWN of food (ENZYMATIC DIGESTION) into small molecules (sugars, amino acids, fatty acids) that can be absorbed. They also lubricate food (mucus), neutralise acid (bicarbonate) and help protect the gut lining.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Producing red blood cells — Red blood cells are produced in the bone marrow.
• Regulating body temperature exclusively — Body temperature is regulated by the hypothalamus, skin and circulation, not GI secretions.
• Filtering blood — Blood is filtered by the kidneys (and the liver and spleen in other ways).

⭐ Key point: Large losses of GI secretions (vomiting, NG drainage, diarrhoea, fistulas) cause dehydration and electrolyte/acid-base disturbances.$x$, null),
(11, $x$What proportion of resting cardiac output goes to the brain?$x$, $x$15%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 15%
Although the BRAIN is only about 2% of body weight, it receives about 15% of the RESTING CARDIAC OUTPUT (about 750 mL/min) and uses about 20% of the body's oxygen. It has no fuel stores, so it depends on a continuous supply of blood, oxygen and glucose. Loss of blood flow causes unconsciousness within seconds and irreversible damage within minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5% — 5% is too low.
• 10% — 10% is too low.
• 20% — 20% is the proportion of the body's OXYGEN consumption, not cardiac output.

⭐ Key point: Brain blood flow depends on cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP − ICP); maintain MAP and control ICP in brain-injured patients.$x$, null),
(12, $x$The following glial cells are located in the central nervous system EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Schwann cell$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Schwann cell
GLIAL CELLS support and protect neurons. In the CENTRAL NERVOUS SYSTEM (brain and spinal cord): ASTROCYTES (support, blood–brain barrier), OLIGODENDROCYTES (make myelin in the CNS), MICROGLIA (immune cells), and EPENDYMAL cells (line the ventricles and help produce CSF). In the PERIPHERAL NERVOUS SYSTEM: SCHWANN CELLS (make myelin around peripheral nerves) and satellite cells. So the Schwann cell is the exception.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ependymal cell — Ependymal cells ARE CNS glia.
• Astrocyte — Astrocytes ARE CNS glia.
• Microglia — Microglia ARE CNS glia.

⭐ Key point: Guillain–Barré syndrome damages Schwann cell myelin in peripheral nerves; multiple sclerosis damages oligodendrocyte myelin in the CNS.$x$, null),
(13, $x$Fluid movement out of capillaries into the interstitium is favoured by:$x$, $x$High capillary hydrostatic pressure and low plasma oncotic pressure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: High capillary hydrostatic pressure and low plasma oncotic pressure
Fluid movement across capillary walls depends on STARLING FORCES: CAPILLARY HYDROSTATIC PRESSURE (the blood pressure inside capillaries) PUSHES fluid OUT; PLASMA ONCOTIC PRESSURE (from proteins, mainly albumin) PULLS fluid back IN. So fluid moves OUT into the tissues (causing oedema) when HYDROSTATIC pressure is HIGH (e.g. heart failure, fluid overload) and/or ONCOTIC pressure is LOW (e.g. low albumin in liver disease, nephrotic syndrome, malnutrition). Leaky capillaries (sepsis, burns) also increase outflow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low capillary hydrostatic pressure only — Low hydrostatic pressure alone would reduce fluid leaving the capillaries.
• High plasma oncotic pressure only — High plasma oncotic pressure pulls fluid INTO capillaries.
• Equal hydrostatic and oncotic pressures — Equal pressures would produce little net movement.

⭐ Key point: Oedema mechanisms: high hydrostatic pressure, low oncotic pressure, increased capillary permeability, and lymphatic blockage.$x$, null),
(14, $x$The functional unit of the kidney responsible for filtration is the:$x$, $x$Nephron$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Nephron
The NEPHRON is the FUNCTIONAL UNIT of the kidney — each kidney has about 1 million. Each nephron has a GLOMERULUS (a ball of capillaries where blood is FILTERED), Bowman's capsule, proximal tubule, loop of Henle, distal tubule, and collecting duct (where water, salts and wastes are reabsorbed or secreted to form urine).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal pelvis — The renal pelvis collects urine from the calyces — it does not filter.
• Ureter — The ureter carries urine to the bladder.
• Bladder trigone — The bladder trigone is a triangular area of the bladder wall.

⭐ Key point: The glomerular filtration rate (GFR, normal about 90–120 mL/min) measures how well nephrons filter; it falls in kidney disease.$x$, null),
(15, $x$The fluid that passes through the lymphatic vessels$x$, $x$Moves in a single direction toward the heart$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Moves in a single direction toward the heart
LYMPH is fluid that leaks out of blood capillaries into the tissues and is collected by LYMPHATIC VESSELS. Lymph flows in ONE DIRECTION ONLY — TOWARDS THE HEART — helped by VALVES inside the vessels (which prevent backflow), muscle contractions and breathing movements. It passes through lymph nodes (which filter it) and finally returns to the blood through the THORACIC DUCT (into the left subclavian vein) and the right lymphatic duct (into the right subclavian vein).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flows toward the lungs — Lymph flows towards the heart and veins, not the lungs.
• Passes from the lymphatic vessels into the arteries — Lymph enters VEINS, not arteries.
• Enters the left ventricle of the heart through the right thoracic duct — Lymph does not enter the left ventricle; it enters the venous system near the heart.

⭐ Key point: Blocked lymphatics cause lymphoedema (e.g. after lymph node removal in breast cancer surgery).$x$, null),
(16, $x$During a blood transfusion, the patient's RR is 24 and BP 90/60. The nurse's priority is to:$x$, $x$Stop the transfusion immediately$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stop the transfusion immediately
A rising RESPIRATORY RATE (24/min) and FALLING BP (90/60) during a blood transfusion suggest a possible SERIOUS TRANSFUSION REACTION — e.g. acute haemolytic reaction, anaphylaxis, sepsis from contaminated blood, or TRALI. The PRIORITY is to STOP THE TRANSFUSION IMMEDIATELY to prevent more of the reacting blood from entering. Then keep the vein open with 0.9% saline through a NEW giving set, check vital signs and patient ID, and call the doctor.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Slow the infusion rate — Slowing the infusion still allows more harmful blood to enter.
• Flush the line with normal saline through the same giving set — Flushing through the SAME set pushes more donor blood into the patient.
• Notify the doctor and continue — Continuing while notifying the doctor delays stopping the reaction.

⭐ Key point: Return the blood bag and giving set to the blood bank, and send blood and urine samples as per protocol.$x$, null),
(17, $x$The first step in the recommended 5-step ABG interpretation approach is to assess:$x$, $x$Oxygenation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Oxygenation
A common systematic approach to ABG interpretation starts by assessing OXYGENATION (PaO2, SpO2, and the P/F ratio) — because hypoxaemia is the most immediately life-threatening problem — before looking at acid-base balance. The usual steps: (1) Oxygenation; (2) pH (acidosis or alkalosis); (3) PaCO2 (respiratory component); (4) HCO3⁻/base excess (metabolic component); (5) Compensation (and anion gap if metabolic acidosis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Compensation — Compensation is assessed near the end.
• Anion gap — The anion gap is calculated when there is metabolic acidosis.
• Base excess only — Base excess is part of the metabolic assessment, not the first step.

⭐ Key point: Always interpret ABGs together with the patient's clinical condition and the FiO2 they are receiving.$x$, null),
(18, $x$A commonly used massive transfusion protocol ratio for packed red cells, plasma, and platelets is approximately:$x$, $x$1:1:1$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1:1:1
In MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE (e.g. major trauma), giving only red cells and saline dilutes clotting factors and platelets, worsening bleeding. MASSIVE TRANSFUSION PROTOCOLS give PACKED RED CELLS, PLASMA (FFP) and PLATELETS in a ratio of about 1:1:1 (balanced resuscitation), which more closely replaces whole blood and improves survival (PROPPR trial). Cryoprecipitate (fibrinogen), calcium and tranexamic acid are also given.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10:1:1 — 10:1:1 gives far too little plasma and platelets.
• 1:10:1 — 1:10:1 gives far too much plasma.
• 5:5:1 — 5:5:1 gives too few platelets.

⭐ Key point: Watch for complications of massive transfusion: hypothermia, hypocalcaemia (citrate), hyperkalaemia, acidosis, and TRALI/TACO.$x$, null),
(19, $x$What is the primary purpose of administering potassium chloride infusion to patient with diabetic ketoacidosis recieving IV insulin?$x$, $x$Replacement of potassium losses$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Replacement of potassium losses
In DKA, total body POTASSIUM is LOW (lost in urine through osmotic diuresis and vomiting), even if the blood level looks normal or high at first (acidosis and lack of insulin shift potassium OUT of cells). When IV INSULIN is given, it drives potassium back INTO the cells, so the blood potassium can FALL quickly and dangerously. POTASSIUM CHLORIDE infusion is given to REPLACE POTASSIUM LOSSES and prevent hypokalaemia (which can cause arrhythmias and muscle weakness).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treatment of hypepnea — "Hyperpnoea" (deep breathing) is due to acidosis, not treated with potassium.
• Prevention of fluccid paralysis — Preventing flaccid paralysis is a consequence of preventing hypokalaemia, but the primary purpose is replacement.
• Treatment of cardiac arrythmias — Treating arrhythmias is not the primary purpose; preventing hypokalaemia prevents them.

⭐ Key point: Check potassium before starting insulin; if potassium is below 3.3 mmol/L, give potassium first and delay insulin.$x$, null),
(20, $x$A patient develops urticaria and itching during a blood transfusion. The nurse should:$x$, $x$A and B$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A and B
URTICARIA (hives) and ITCHING during a transfusion indicate a MILD ALLERGIC reaction (to plasma proteins in the donor blood). The nurse should STOP (pause) THE TRANSFUSION and ADMINISTER an ANTIHISTAMINE such as DIPHENHYDRAMINE (or chlorphenamine), as prescribed. If symptoms resolve completely and there are no other signs (no fever, breathing difficulty or hypotension), the doctor may allow the transfusion to restart slowly. So "A and B" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop the transfusion — Stopping alone is incomplete; the symptoms also need treatment.
• Administer diphenhydramine — An antihistamine alone without pausing the transfusion is unsafe.
• Slow the infusion rate — Slowing the rate is not enough.

⭐ Key point: If wheeze, swelling or hypotension develop, treat as anaphylaxis (IM adrenaline) and do not restart.$x$, null),
(21, $x$A key early clinical sign that should raise suspicion of DIC in a critically ill patient is:$x$, $x$Unexplained oozing from IV/puncture sites alongside signs of organ dysfunction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Unexplained oozing from IV/puncture sites alongside signs of organ dysfunction
DISSEMINATED INTRAVASCULAR COAGULATION (DIC) is widespread activation of clotting in critical illness (sepsis, trauma, obstetric emergencies, cancer). Clotting factors and platelets are used up, causing BLEEDING. A KEY EARLY SIGN is UNEXPLAINED OOZING from IV sites, puncture sites, wounds or gums, ALONGSIDE ORGAN DYSFUNCTION (from tiny clots blocking small vessels — e.g. kidney injury, confusion, hypoxia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Isolated mild headache with no other findings — An isolated mild headache is not suggestive.
• Normal coagulation results with no bleeding — Normal clotting with no bleeding does not suggest DIC.
• Bradycardia alone — Bradycardia alone is not suggestive.

⭐ Key point: Investigate with platelets, PT/APTT, fibrinogen and D-dimer; treat the underlying cause.$x$, null),
(22, $x$The extrinsic coagulation pathway is activated by:$x$, $x$Tissue factor released from damaged tissue$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tissue factor released from damaged tissue
The EXTRINSIC coagulation pathway is activated by TISSUE FACTOR (TF, thromboplastin), released from DAMAGED TISSUE and blood vessel walls. Tissue factor combines with FACTOR VII, which then activates factor X, leading to thrombin and a clot. This pathway acts quickly after injury. It is measured by the PT/INR.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Contact with collagen — Contact with collagen (exposed surfaces) activates the INTRINSIC pathway (factor XII).
• Platelet aggregation alone — Platelet aggregation is part of primary haemostasis.
• Fibrinolysis — Fibrinolysis breaks down clots.

⭐ Key point: Warfarin mainly affects the extrinsic pathway (factor VII), so it is monitored with INR.$x$, null),
(23, $x$A fall in HCO3 causing acidosis indicates the disorder is:$x$, $x$Metabolic$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic
Acid–base balance has two parts: the RESPIRATORY part, controlled by the lungs and shown by PaCO2, and the METABOLIC part, controlled mainly by the kidneys and shown by BICARBONATE (HCO3⁻, normal 22–26 mmol/L). Bicarbonate is the body's main BASE (buffer). When HCO3⁻ FALLS and the pH falls (acidosis), the problem is METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. Causes: GAIN of acid (lactic acidosis in shock or sepsis, ketoacidosis in diabetes or starvation, kidney failure, poisons such as methanol or aspirin) or LOSS of bicarbonate (diarrhoea, ileostomy, renal tubular acidosis). The lungs try to compensate by fast, deep breathing to blow off CO2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory — A RESPIRATORY disorder is caused by a change in PaCO2, not bicarbonate.
• Purely a lab error — A low bicarbonate with acidosis is a real, common finding — not a lab error.
• Always fatal — Metabolic acidosis is treatable and not always fatal; outcome depends on the cause.

⭐ Key point: In metabolic acidosis, calculate the ANION GAP (Na⁺ − [Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻]) — a high gap suggests added acids (lactate, ketones, toxins); a normal gap suggests bicarbonate loss.$x$, null),
(24, $x$Which clinical manifestation would lead the nurse to suspect that a client is experiencing hypermagnesemia?$x$, $x$Hot flushed skin and diaphoresis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hot flushed skin and diaphoresis
HYPERMAGNESAEMIA (high magnesium) causes VASODILATION and depresses nerves and muscles. Early signs include FLUSHING, a sensation of WARMTH, sweating (DIAPHORESIS), nausea and hypotension. As levels rise: loss of deep tendon reflexes, drowsiness, muscle weakness, respiratory depression, bradycardia and cardiac arrest. Common causes: magnesium sulphate therapy (pre-eclampsia), kidney failure, and magnesium-containing antacids or laxatives.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased respiratory rate and depth — Respiratory rate and depth DECREASE in hypermagnesaemia.
• Soft-tissue calcification and hyperreflexia — Hyperreflexia is a feature of HYPOmagnesaemia; hypermagnesaemia causes HYPOreflexia.
• Muscle pain and acute rhabdomyolysis — Muscle pain and rhabdomyolysis are not typical.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV calcium gluconate (antidote), stop magnesium, give fluids and diuretics, or dialysis in kidney failure.$x$, null),
(25, $x$Elevated ALT and AST primarily indicate:$x$, $x$Hepatocellular injury$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hepatocellular injury
ALT (alanine aminotransferase) and AST (aspartate aminotransferase) are enzymes found inside LIVER CELLS (hepatocytes). When liver cells are damaged, they leak into the blood, so RAISED ALT and AST indicate HEPATOCELLULAR INJURY — e.g. viral hepatitis, paracetamol overdose, ischaemic ("shock") liver, alcoholic or drug-induced liver injury. ALT is more specific to the liver; AST is also found in muscle and heart.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal dysfunction — Kidney function is measured by urea and creatinine.
• Pancreatic dysfunction only — Pancreatic damage is shown by amylase and lipase.
• Coagulopathy directly — Coagulopathy is measured by PT/INR, though liver damage can cause it indirectly.

⭐ Key point: Very high transaminases (in the thousands) suggest acute hepatitis, paracetamol toxicity or shock liver.$x$, null),
(26, $x$The direct Coombs test is used to detect:$x$, $x$Antibodies bound to the surface of red blood cells$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Antibodies bound to the surface of red blood cells
The DIRECT COOMBS TEST (direct antiglobulin test, DAT) detects ANTIBODIES (or complement) already BOUND to the SURFACE of the patient's RED BLOOD CELLS. A positive result indicates IMMUNE-MEDIATED HAEMOLYSIS — e.g. haemolytic transfusion reaction, haemolytic disease of the newborn (Rh or ABO incompatibility), or autoimmune haemolytic anaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Platelet aggregation — Platelet aggregation is tested differently.
• White cell count — White cell count is part of the full blood count.
• Clotting factor deficiency — Clotting factor deficiency is tested with clotting factor assays.

⭐ Key point: The INDIRECT Coombs test detects free antibodies in the plasma (used in pregnancy screening and crossmatching).$x$, null),
(27, $x$A patient with hypernatremia (Na⁺ 160 mEq/L) is lethargic. Which fluid should the nurse administer?$x$, $x$D5W$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: D5W
HYPERNATRAEMIA (sodium 160) usually means the body has lost too much WATER relative to sodium. The brain cells shrink, causing LETHARGY, confusion, seizures and coma. Treatment is to replace the FREE WATER deficit with a HYPOTONIC fluid such as 5% DEXTROSE IN WATER (D5W) — after the glucose is metabolised, it provides pure water. Sodium must be lowered SLOWLY (no more than about 10 mmol/L in 24 hours in chronic cases) to avoid cerebral oedema. (If the patient is shocked/hypovolaemic, isotonic saline is given first to restore circulation.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 3% saline — 3% saline is hypertonic — it would raise sodium further.
• 0.9% saline — 0.9% saline (154 mmol/L) is used for volume resuscitation but provides little free water.
• Lactated Ringer’s — Lactated Ringer's is near-isotonic and doesn't correct a free water deficit efficiently.

⭐ Key point: Monitor sodium every 4–6 hours during correction and watch for neurological changes.$x$, null),
(28, $x$To provide rapid pain relief in the ICU, which of the following routes of opioid administration is most appropriate?$x$, $x$Intravenous$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intravenous
For RAPID PAIN RELIEF in ICU, the INTRAVENOUS (IV) route is most appropriate. IV opioids (e.g. morphine, fentanyl) act within minutes, have predictable absorption (critically ill patients often have poor gut and skin blood flow), and can be titrated easily in small doses or by continuous infusion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intradermal — Intradermal is used for skin tests, not analgesia.
• Intraspinal — Intraspinal (epidural/intrathecal) is effective, but needs specialist insertion — not for rapid general use.
• Sublingual — Sublingual absorption is variable and slower.

⭐ Key point: Assess pain with validated tools (CPOT or BPS in patients who cannot speak) and monitor sedation and breathing.$x$, null),
(29, $x$A patient develops flushing, pruritus, and a rash on the neck and upper torso shortly after a rapid vancomycin infusion. The most appropriate nursing action is to:$x$, $x$Slow the infusion rate, as this is histamine-mediated red man syndrome rather than a true allergic reaction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Slow the infusion rate, as this is histamine-mediated red man syndrome rather than a true allergic reaction
FLUSHING, ITCHING and a RASH on the face, NECK and UPPER TORSO shortly after a RAPID vancomycin infusion is typical of "RED MAN SYNDROME" (vancomycin infusion reaction). It is caused by direct HISTAMINE release from mast cells due to the fast infusion rate — NOT a true IgE allergy. The appropriate action is to STOP or SLOW THE INFUSION (an antihistamine can help), then continue at a slower rate (at least 60 minutes per gram).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop vancomycin permanently and document a severe anaphylactic allergy — It is not a true anaphylactic allergy, so vancomycin doesn't need to be permanently stopped.
• Administer epinephrine immediately — Adrenaline is needed only for true anaphylaxis (airway swelling, wheeze, severe hypotension).
• Increase the infusion rate since the reaction is self-limited — Increasing the rate would worsen the reaction.

⭐ Key point: Document as an "infusion reaction" to avoid wrongly labelling the patient as allergic.$x$, null),
(30, $x$A key nursing precaution when administering IV potassium chloride is to:$x$, $x$Never give it as an undiluted rapid IV push, due to risk of fatal arrhythmia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Never give it as an undiluted rapid IV push, due to risk of fatal arrhythmia
IV POTASSIUM CHLORIDE (KCl) is a HIGH-ALERT medication. Given as an UNDILUTED RAPID IV PUSH, it causes a sudden high potassium level in the heart, leading to FATAL ARRHYTHMIAS and CARDIAC ARREST. It must ALWAYS be DILUTED, given by INFUSION PUMP, at a controlled rate (usually no more than 10 mmol/hr peripherally, up to 20 mmol/hr centrally with ECG monitoring).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give it as a rapid IV push for fast correction — Rapid IV push is never acceptable.
• Administer it intramuscularly for safety — IM potassium is not used.
• Mix it with any other IV drug freely — KCl should not be mixed freely with other drugs.

⭐ Key point: Concentrated KCl ampoules should be stored securely and separately in clinical areas to prevent fatal errors.$x$, null),
(31, $x$A patient with septic shock is started on vasopressin. Which effect is expected?$x$, $x$Vasoconstriction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Vasoconstriction
VASOPRESSIN acts on V1 receptors in blood vessel walls, causing VASOCONSTRICTION. In septic shock, it raises SVR and BP, helping to reduce the dose of noradrenaline needed. It also acts on V2 receptors in the kidneys (water retention).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Decreased SVR — It INCREASES SVR, not decreases it.
• Increased urine output — It may increase urine output in some cases by improving BP, but its main effect is vasoconstriction.
• Coronary vasodilation — It can cause coronary VASOCONSTRICTION (ischaemia risk).

⭐ Key point: Vasopressin dose in septic shock is usually up to 0.03 units/min, not titrated like noradrenaline.$x$, null),
(32, $x$Benzylpenicillin is the chemical name for:$x$, $x$Penicillin G$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Penicillin G
BENZYLPENICILLIN is the chemical (generic) name for PENICILLIN G, the original penicillin discovered by Alexander Fleming. It is destroyed by stomach acid, so it is given by INJECTION (IV or IM). It kills many gram-positive bacteria and some others. Uses include: streptococcal infections (e.g. cellulitis, tonsillitis, rheumatic fever prevention), MENINGOCOCCAL disease (meningitis/septicaemia), pneumococcal pneumonia, syphilis (as benzathine penicillin), tetanus, and early neonatal sepsis (with gentamicin).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Penicillin V — Penicillin V is PHENOXYMETHYLPENICILLIN — the acid-stable ORAL form.
• Penicillin F — "Penicillin F" was an early natural penicillin that is not used clinically.
• Phenethicillin — Phenethicillin is a different, semi-synthetic oral penicillin.

⭐ Key point: Always ask about penicillin allergy before giving it. Have adrenaline ready — anaphylaxis can occur within minutes of injection.$x$, null),
(33, $x$Prophylactic antibiotic use refers to:$x$, $x$Preventing infection before it starts, e.g. before surgery$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Preventing infection before it starts, e.g. before surgery
PROPHYLACTIC antibiotic use means giving antibiotics to PREVENT an infection BEFORE it starts — e.g. before SURGERY (to prevent surgical site infection), after exposure to meningococcal disease, or in high-risk patients (e.g. asplenia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treating an already established infection — Treating an existing infection is THERAPEUTIC use.
• Only used for viral infections — Antibiotics do not treat viral infections.
• A synonym for therapeutic use — Prophylactic and therapeutic use are different.

⭐ Key point: Surgical prophylaxis is usually given IV within 60 minutes before the incision and is usually stopped within 24 hours.$x$, null),
(34, $x$A patient who received naloxone for heroin overdose initially improves but becomes drowsy and hypoventilating again 45 minutes later. What is the most likely explanation?$x$, $x$Naloxone has a shorter half-life than most opioids, so re-sedation occurs as the antidote wears off before the opioid does$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Naloxone has a shorter half-life than most opioids, so re-sedation occurs as the antidote wears off before the opioid does
NALOXONE reverses opioid effects quickly, but it has a SHORT HALF-LIFE (about 30–90 minutes). Many opioids (e.g. heroin, morphine, methadone) last longer. When naloxone wears off, the opioid is still present, causing RE-SEDATION and respiratory depression. Patients must be observed for several hours after naloxone, and repeat doses or an infusion may be needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient has developed an allergic reaction to naloxone — This is not an allergic reaction.
• The initial dose of naloxone was too high — A high dose of naloxone doesn't cause re-sedation.
• This indicates the patient never had an opioid overdose — The improvement with naloxone confirms opioid overdose.

⭐ Key point: Observe patients for at least 2–4 hours after naloxone for short-acting opioids, longer for long-acting ones.$x$, null),
(35, $x$Amiodarone is primarily indicated in cardiac arrest management for:$x$, $x$Shockable rhythms (VF/pulseless VT) refractory to defibrillation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Shockable rhythms (VF/pulseless VT) refractory to defibrillation
In CARDIAC ARREST, AMIODARONE is used for SHOCKABLE RHYTHMS — VENTRICULAR FIBRILLATION (VF) and PULSELESS VENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA (pVT) — that persist after defibrillation (refractory). The dose is 300 mg IV after the third shock, then 150 mg after the fifth shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Asystole only — Asystole is a non-shockable rhythm; amiodarone is not indicated.
• Bradycardia — Bradycardia is treated with atropine, pacing, or adrenaline infusion.
• Hypertensive emergency — Hypertensive emergency is treated with antihypertensives.

⭐ Key point: High-quality CPR and early defibrillation are the most important treatments in VF/pVT.$x$, null),
(36, $x$All are indications for Adrenaline use EXCEPT$x$, $x$Heart failure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Heart failure
ADRENALINE (epinephrine) is used in: CARDIAC RESUSCITATION (1 mg IV every 3–5 minutes), ANAPHYLAXIS (0.5 mg IM in adults), and SYMPTOMATIC BRADYCARDIA (as an infusion if atropine fails). It is NOT a standard treatment for HEART FAILURE (it increases heart workload and oxygen demand; although it may be used as an inotrope in cardiogenic shock, it is not a routine heart failure treatment).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cardiac resuscitation — Cardiac resuscitation IS an indication.
• Anaphylaxis — Anaphylaxis IS an indication.
• Symptomatic bradycardia — Symptomatic bradycardia IS an indication.

⭐ Key point: Know the correct route and dose for each indication — IV adrenaline in anaphylaxis is dangerous outside of cardiac arrest or specialist settings.$x$, null),
(37, $x$Amiodarone therapy requires monitoring of which organs due to potential toxicity?$x$, $x$Thyroid, liver, and lungs$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Thyroid, liver, and lungs
AMIODARONE can damage several organs with long-term use: THYROID (hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism — it contains iodine), LIVER (raised liver enzymes, hepatitis), and LUNGS (pulmonary fibrosis, pneumonitis). Monitoring of thyroid function, liver function and lung function (chest X-ray) is required. It can also affect the eyes (corneal deposits) and skin (grey-blue colour, photosensitivity).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the kidneys — Kidneys are not the main concern.
• Only the pancreas — The pancreas is not the main concern.
• Only the skin — Skin effects occur but are not the only concern.

⭐ Key point: Amiodarone interacts with warfarin and digoxin — reduce their doses.$x$, null),
(38, $x$Vasopressin is sometimes added in septic shock management primarily to:$x$, $x$Allow a norepinephrine-sparing effect by acting on a different receptor pathway$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Allow a norepinephrine-sparing effect by acting on a different receptor pathway
In SEPTIC SHOCK, VASOPRESSIN is added to NORADRENALINE when BP remains low despite moderate doses. It acts on a different receptor pathway (V1 receptors), so it raises BP by a different mechanism, allowing a lower noradrenaline dose ("NORADRENALINE-SPARING effect"). Septic shock may also cause a relative vasopressin deficiency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replace norepinephrine entirely — It doesn't replace noradrenaline entirely.
• Treat bradycardia — It doesn't treat bradycardia.
• Lower blood pressure — It raises, not lowers, BP.

⭐ Key point: Adding vasopressin is recommended when noradrenaline reaches 0.25–0.5 mcg/kg/min.$x$, null),
(39, $x$Neuromuscular blocking agents (e.g. vecuronium) must always be administered together with:$x$, $x$Adequate sedation and analgesia, since paralytics do not sedate or relieve pain$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Adequate sedation and analgesia, since paralytics do not sedate or relieve pain
NEUROMUSCULAR BLOCKING AGENTS (e.g. vecuronium, rocuronium, cisatracurium) PARALYSE muscles but do NOT provide SEDATION or PAIN RELIEF. A paralysed patient may be fully awake and in pain without being able to show it — a terrifying experience. They must ALWAYS be given with ADEQUATE SEDATION and ANALGESIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nothing else is required — Sedation and analgesia are essential.
• A diuretic — Diuretics are not required.
• An antihistamine — Antihistamines are not required.

⭐ Key point: Monitor depth of paralysis with train-of-four, provide eye care and pressure care.$x$, null),
(40, $x$Fentanyl is often preferred over morphine in patients with renal impairment because fentanyl:$x$, $x$Has no clinically significant active metabolites that accumulate in renal failure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Has no clinically significant active metabolites that accumulate in renal failure
MORPHINE is broken down into active metabolites (e.g. morphine-6-glucuronide), which are excreted by the kidneys and ACCUMULATE in RENAL FAILURE, causing prolonged sedation and respiratory depression. FENTANYL is broken down in the liver into INACTIVE metabolites, so it does not accumulate in renal failure. This makes fentanyl safer in renal impairment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Has active metabolites that accumulate in renal failure — Fentanyl does NOT have active metabolites that accumulate.
• Cannot be given intravenously — Fentanyl can be given IV.
• Has a much longer half-life — Fentanyl has a shorter half-life after a single dose (though it accumulates in fat with long infusions).

⭐ Key point: Other opioids that are relatively safe in renal failure: alfentanil and remifentanil.$x$, null),
(41, $x$Which of the following is classified as a positive (radio-opaque/white-appearing) contrast agent?$x$, $x$Barium$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Barium
CONTRAST AGENTS make structures easier to see on X-ray or CT. POSITIVE (radio-opaque) contrast agents contain heavy atoms that ABSORB X-RAYS strongly, so they appear WHITE on images. Examples: BARIUM sulphate (swallowed or given as an enema to outline the GI tract) and IODINE-based contrast (injected IV for CT scans, angiography, IVU). NEGATIVE (radiolucent) contrast agents such as AIR or CARBON DIOXIDE absorb few X-rays and appear DARK (black); they are sometimes used together with barium in "double-contrast" studies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Room air — Room air is a NEGATIVE contrast agent (appears dark).
• Carbon dioxide — Carbon dioxide is a NEGATIVE contrast agent (appears dark).
• Vacuum/no contrast — "No contrast" means no agent is used.

⭐ Key point: Before iodine contrast: check kidney function, ask about previous contrast reactions and allergies, and ensure hydration. Barium should not be used when bowel perforation is suspected.$x$, null),
(42, $x$After a pneumonectomy, the trachea characteristically shifts:$x$, $x$Toward the operated (empty) side$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Toward the operated (empty) side
After a PNEUMONECTOMY (removal of a whole lung), the empty space fills with fluid over time, and the MEDIASTINUM and TRACHEA shift TOWARDS the OPERATED (empty) side, because there is no lung there to keep them in place. This is expected.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Away from the operated side — Shift AWAY from the operated side would suggest something is pushing (e.g. excessive fluid or bleeding).
• Never shifts after pneumonectomy — The trachea does shift after pneumonectomy.
• Shifts downward only — The trachea doesn't just shift downward.

⭐ Key point: A sudden shift AWAY from the operated side may indicate bleeding or bronchopleural fistula — report urgently.$x$, null),
(43, $x$A patient with a suspected small bowel obstruction has a CT scan with oral contrast ordered. The nurse understands the choice of a water-soluble (rather than barium) contrast agent in this context is because:$x$, $x$If perforation is present, water-soluble contrast is safer than barium, which causes severe chemical peritonitis if it leaks into the peritoneum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: If perforation is present, water-soluble contrast is safer than barium, which causes severe chemical peritonitis if it leaks into the peritoneum
If there is a PERFORATION, BARIUM leaking into the peritoneal cavity causes severe CHEMICAL PERITONITIS and adhesions (it is not absorbed). WATER-SOLUBLE contrast (e.g. Gastrografin) is absorbed and causes much less harm if it leaks, so it is SAFER when perforation is possible.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Water-soluble contrast is always cheaper — Cost is not the reason.
• Barium provides better image quality in every situation — Barium does not always give better images.
• There is no difference in safety between the two agents — There is a significant difference in safety.

⭐ Key point: Water-soluble contrast can also help resolve adhesive small bowel obstruction (diagnostic and therapeutic).$x$, null),
(44, $x$The air bronchogram sign on chest X-ray is most associated with:$x$, $x$Consolidation/pneumonia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Consolidation/pneumonia
An AIR BRONCHOGRAM appears as dark, branching tubes (air-filled bronchi) seen against a WHITE background of lung. Normally, bronchi are not visible because the surrounding lung is also full of air (both dark). When the ALVEOLI around the bronchi become filled with FLUID, PUS, blood or cells (CONSOLIDATION), the lung turns white, and the air-filled bronchi stand out. It is classic for PNEUMONIA, and is also seen in pulmonary oedema, ARDS and lung haemorrhage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pneumothorax — A pneumothorax shows a dark area with NO lung markings and a visible lung edge.
• Rib fracture — A rib fracture shows a break or step in the bone outline.
• Cardiomegaly — Cardiomegaly shows an enlarged heart shadow (cardiothoracic ratio above 0.5).

⭐ Key point: Consolidation + air bronchograms + fever and cough strongly suggests pneumonia — take sputum and blood cultures and start antibiotics.$x$, null),
(45, $x$A widened mediastinum on chest X-ray after trauma is most suggestive of:$x$, $x$Thoracic aortic disruption$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Thoracic aortic disruption
After major blunt trauma (especially HIGH-SPEED DECELERATION — head-on collisions or falls from height), a WIDENED MEDIASTINUM on chest X-ray (more than about 8 cm on a supine AP film) suggests bleeding into the mediastinum from a THORACIC AORTIC DISRUPTION (tear), most commonly at the aortic isthmus just beyond the left subclavian artery. Other X-ray clues: loss of the aortic knob, deviation of the trachea or NG tube to the right, left apical "cap", and fractures of the first/second ribs. Most patients with complete transection die at the scene; survivors have a contained tear that can rupture at any moment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple rib fracture — A simple rib fracture does not widen the mediastinum.
• Pleural effusion — A pleural effusion causes fluid in the pleural space, not mediastinal widening.
• Diaphragmatic hernia — A diaphragmatic hernia shows bowel or stomach in the chest.

⭐ Key point: Confirm with CT angiography; keep heart rate under 100 and systolic BP about 100–120 mmHg (beta-blocker), give analgesia, and arrange urgent surgical or endovascular repair.$x$, null),
(46, $x$WHICH OF THESE BEST DESCRIBES A COMPUTER$x$, $x$An electronic device that accepts data, processes and stores it, and gives out information$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An electronic device that accepts data, processes and stores it, and gives out information
A COMPUTER is an ELECTRONIC DEVICE that ACCEPTS DATA (input), PROCESSES it (according to instructions), STORES it, and GIVES OUT INFORMATION (output).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A device used only for playing games — Computers are used for much more than games.
• The means of connection between a sender and a receiver — "The means of connection between sender and receiver" describes a communication medium.
• A collection of data being transmitted — "A collection of data being transmitted" describes a message.

⭐ Key point: The four basic functions: input, processing, output, and storage.$x$, null),
(47, $x$Random Access Memory (RAM) differs from Read Only Memory (ROM) in that RAM is:$x$, $x$Volatile — its contents are lost when power is off$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Volatile — its contents are lost when power is off
RAM (Random Access Memory) is VOLATILE — its contents are LOST when the power is turned off. It stores the data and programs the computer is currently using. ROM (Read Only Memory) is NON-VOLATILE — it keeps its contents (e.g. startup instructions) without power.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Permanent and non-volatile — RAM is not permanent; ROM is.
• Used only for long-term archival storage — RAM is not used for long-term storage.
• Not involved in program execution — RAM is essential for running programs.

⭐ Key point: Save work frequently to avoid losing data in power cuts.$x$, null),
(48, $x$A nurse is setting up a new computer station in a clinic. Which of the following is considered an output device?$x$, $x$Printer$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Printer
OUTPUT DEVICES take the processed information from the computer and present it to the user in a usable form. A PRINTER produces a permanent paper copy ("hard copy") — e.g. printing an ECG strip, a lab report, a drug chart, or a discharge summary. Other output devices: MONITOR/screen ("soft copy"), speakers, headphones and projectors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mouse — A MOUSE is an INPUT device — it sends pointing and clicking commands INTO the computer.
• Scanner — A SCANNER is an INPUT device — it converts paper documents or images into digital data.
• Microphone — A MICROPHONE is an INPUT device — it sends sound into the computer.

⭐ Key point: Some devices are both input and output (e.g. touchscreens, multifunction printer-scanners).$x$, null),
(49, $x$A phishing email pretending to be from IT asking staff to 'confirm' their password is an example of:$x$, $x$A social engineering cybersecurity threat$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A social engineering cybersecurity threat
PHISHING is a cyber-attack in which criminals send messages (emails, texts, calls) that PRETEND to come from a TRUSTED SOURCE — such as the hospital IT department, a bank or a manager — to trick people into giving away PASSWORDS, clicking harmful links or opening infected attachments. This is SOCIAL ENGINEERING: it attacks human trust rather than computer systems. Stolen login details can be used to access confidential patient records or install ransomware that locks hospital systems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A legitimate security update — Genuine IT departments NEVER ask staff to send or "confirm" passwords by email.
• A required annual login refresh — There is no legitimate "annual login refresh" requiring you to send your password.
• A harmless marketing email — Phishing is NOT harmless — it can lead to data breaches and system shutdown.

⭐ Key point: Check the sender's real address, don't click suspicious links, never share passwords, and report suspicious emails to IT immediately.$x$, null),
(50, $x$Which of the following best describes 'utility software'?$x$, $x$Software designed to perform maintenance tasks such as antivirus scanning or disk cleanup$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Software designed to perform maintenance tasks such as antivirus scanning or disk cleanup
UTILITY SOFTWARE is a type of system support software that performs MAINTENANCE and housekeeping tasks to keep the computer secure, efficient and working well. Examples: ANTIVIRUS/anti-malware programs (detect and remove viruses), DISK CLEANUP and defragmentation tools, BACKUP software, file compression (e.g. zip), firewall software and system monitoring tools.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Software used only to write documents — Writing documents is done with APPLICATION software (word processors such as Microsoft Word).
• A type of physical computer hardware — Utility software is a program (software), not physical hardware.
• A programming language — A programming language (e.g. Python, Java) is used to write software, not a utility.

⭐ Key point: Keep antivirus software updated and back up important data regularly — hospital computers hold sensitive patient information.$x$, null),
(51, $x$The nurse assessing a ventilated patient should evaluate for signs of inadequate oxygenation or ventilation including all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Polyuria$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Polyuria
Signs of INADEQUATE OXYGENATION or VENTILATION include: RESTLESSNESS and CONFUSION (early sign of brain hypoxia or high CO2), TACHYCARDIA (early compensation), tachypnoea, sweating, cyanosis, and LATE signs such as BRADYCARDIA and HYPOTENSION (pre-arrest). POLYURIA (excessive urine) is NOT a sign of inadequate oxygenation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Restlessness and confusion — Restlessness and confusion ARE signs.
• Tachycardia — Tachycardia IS a sign.
• Bradycardia and hypotension as late signs — Bradycardia and hypotension ARE late signs.

⭐ Key point: Restlessness in a ventilated patient may be hypoxia — check the patient before increasing sedation.$x$, null),
(52, $x$A patient on oxygen therapy complains of a dry nose and throat. The nurse should:$x$, $x$Add a humidifier to the oxygen system$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Add a humidifier to the oxygen system
OXYGEN from cylinders or wall supplies is very DRY. It dries the nose and throat, causing discomfort, nosebleeds and thick secretions. The nurse should ADD A HUMIDIFIER to the oxygen system (especially at higher flows), and provide mouth care and water-based lubricant for the lips/nose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discontinue the oxygen therapy — Stopping oxygen may cause hypoxia.
• Increase the oxygen flow rate — Increasing flow would worsen dryness.
• Switch the patient to a non-rebreather mask — Switching masks doesn't address dryness.

⭐ Key point: Low-flow nasal cannula (below 4 L/min) usually doesn't need humidification; higher flows do.$x$, null),
(53, $x$A patient with acute pulmonary oedema on strict intake and output monitoring produces only 20 mL of urine in 2 hours despite IV furosemide 80 mg. What is the priority nursing action?$x$, $x$Assess for hypotension and reassess renal perfusion, and notify the physician$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assess for hypotension and reassess renal perfusion, and notify the physician
Only 20 mL of urine in 2 hours (about 10 mL/hr) despite IV furosemide 80 mg suggests the kidneys are not responding — possibly due to HYPOTENSION (poor kidney perfusion), AKI, or a blocked catheter. The priority is to ASSESS for HYPOTENSION, REASSESS RENAL PERFUSION (BP, catheter patency, fluid status) and NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer another diuretic dose without further assessment — Giving more diuretic without assessment may worsen hypotension.
• Increase IV fluids to promote diuresis — More IV fluids could worsen pulmonary oedema.
• Discontinue oxygen therapy — Discontinuing oxygen is dangerous in pulmonary oedema.

⭐ Key point: Check the catheter for blockage first.$x$, null),
(54, $x$Kussmaul respiration — deep, rapid breathing — is a compensatory pattern most associated with:$x$, $x$Metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic acidosis
KUSSMAUL RESPIRATION is deep, rapid, laboured breathing ("air hunger"). It is the lungs' COMPENSATION for METABOLIC ACIDOSIS: by breathing deeply and quickly, the body BLOWS OFF CARBON DIOXIDE (an acid), which helps RAISE the blood pH towards normal. It is classically seen in DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS (often with a fruity/acetone smell on the breath), and also in severe kidney failure (uraemic acidosis), lactic acidosis and some poisonings (methanol, ethylene glycol, aspirin).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis is caused by HYPOventilation (CO2 retention), not deep fast breathing.
• Metabolic alkalosis exclusively — Metabolic alkalosis causes SLOW, shallow breathing (to retain CO2).
• Normal resting breathing — Kussmaul breathing is abnormal; normal resting breathing is quiet and regular at 12–20/min.

⭐ Key point: A patient with Kussmaul breathing needs an urgent blood gas, glucose, ketones and lactate.$x$, null),
(55, $x$A patient is inhaling 500 ml of air with 75% Fio2, which of the following term the nurse should document for the amount of air inhaled?$x$, $x$tidal volume$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: tidal volume
TIDAL VOLUME (Vt) is the VOLUME of air INHALED (or exhaled) in ONE NORMAL BREATH — about 500 mL in an average adult (about 6–8 mL/kg). Here, the patient inhales 500 mL per breath, so this is the tidal volume. The FiO2 (75%) describes the OXYGEN CONCENTRATION of that air, not the volume. On a ventilator, tidal volume is set (in volume modes) or monitored (in pressure modes), aiming for 6–8 mL/kg of predicted body weight.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• minute volume — MINUTE VOLUME is tidal volume × respiratory rate (air moved per minute).
• residual volume — RESIDUAL VOLUME is the air left in the lungs after a maximal breath out.
• total lung capacity — TOTAL LUNG CAPACITY is the maximum volume of air the lungs can hold (about 6 L).

⭐ Key point: Low tidal volumes with a fast rate (rapid shallow breathing) are a sign of respiratory fatigue.$x$, null),
(56, $x$This is the similarity between pulmonary and systemic circulation$x$, $x$volume of the circulation per minute$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: volume of the circulation per minute
The PULMONARY and SYSTEMIC circulations are connected in series, so the VOLUME of blood pumped per MINUTE (CARDIAC OUTPUT) by the right ventricle (to the lungs) and the left ventricle (to the body) must be the SAME (about 5 L/min). Otherwise blood would build up in one circulation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• total capacity — Total capacity differs (systemic holds much more blood).
• pulse pressure — Pulse pressure differs (pulmonary pressures are much lower).
• peripheral vascular resistance — Vascular resistance differs (pulmonary resistance is much lower).

⭐ Key point: Pulmonary circulation is a low-pressure system (about 25/10 mmHg).$x$, null),
(57, $x$A patient's chest tube water seal chamber shows fluid that stops fluctuating ('swinging') with respiration. This most likely indicates:$x$, $x$The lung has fully re-expanded, or the tube is blocked/kinked$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The lung has fully re-expanded, or the tube is blocked/kinked
In a chest drain system, fluid in the WATER SEAL chamber normally FLUCTUATES ("SWINGS") up and down with breathing. If the swinging STOPS, it means either the LUNG has FULLY RE-EXPANDED (no space left), or the TUBE is BLOCKED or KINKED.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal ongoing function — It is not normal ongoing function.
• An air leak — An air leak causes bubbling.
• Excessive suction — Excessive suction would cause continuous bubbling in the suction chamber.

⭐ Key point: If swinging stops, assess the patient (breath sounds, SpO2) and check the tubing for kinks and clots.$x$, null),
(58, $x$An open (sucking) chest wound should be managed with:$x$, $x$A 3-sided occlusive dressing$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A 3-sided occlusive dressing
An OPEN (SUCKING) CHEST WOUND lets air into the chest during breathing. It is managed with a 3-SIDED OCCLUSIVE DRESSING (taped on three sides). This acts as a flutter valve: it seals during inspiration, preventing air entry, and lets air escape during expiration, preventing a tension pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A fully sealed 4-sided occlusive dressing — A 4-sided sealed dressing could trap air and cause a tension pneumothorax.
• No dressing at all — No dressing allows air in.
• Direct pressure only, no dressing — Direct pressure alone is not adequate.

⭐ Key point: Commercial vented chest seals are also used. A chest drain is the definitive treatment.$x$, null),
(59, $x$Disadvantages of endotracheal intubation includes all of the following EXCEPT$x$, $x$Decreased airway resistance$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Decreased airway resistance
Disadvantages of ENDOTRACHEAL INTUBATION include: LOSS of the upper airway's PROTECTIVE functions (warming, humidifying, filtering air, cough), LOSS of PHONATION (cannot speak), and DAMAGE to the SUBGLOTTIC area (stenosis, ulceration). DECREASED AIRWAY RESISTANCE is NOT a disadvantage — the tube actually INCREASES resistance compared with the natural airway.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Loss of the protective function of the upper airway — Loss of protective function IS a disadvantage.
• Loss of phonation (voice) — Loss of phonation IS a disadvantage.
• Damage to the subglottic area — Subglottic damage IS a disadvantage.

⭐ Key point: Provide humidification and alternative communication methods for intubated patients.$x$, null),
(60, $x$A patient who has severe shortness of breath(SOB) and his/her ABGs shows high level of carbon dioxide (CO2) 55mm hg and low level of (Sao2) 40mmhg, which shows type II respiratory failure. Which Approach is best to treat the patient?$x$, $x$Delivering oxygen through BIPAP machine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Delivering oxygen through BIPAP machine
TYPE II RESPIRATORY FAILURE (high CO2, low O2) requires ventilatory support to remove CO2. BiPAP (bilevel positive airway pressure) provides higher inspiratory pressure (to increase tidal volume and blow off CO2) and lower expiratory pressure (to keep airways open). It is the best non-invasive approach.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delivering Oxygen through face mask — A face mask alone doesn't remove CO2 and may worsen hypercapnia.
• Delivering oxygen through CPAP machine — CPAP improves oxygenation but doesn't assist ventilation as effectively.
• None of them — BiPAP is an appropriate option.

⭐ Key point: BiPAP reduces intubation and mortality in COPD exacerbations with acidosis.$x$, null),
(61, $x$The dexamethasone dose sometimes used as an alternative to prednisolone in acute asthma is:$x$, $x$0.6mg/kg/day$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 0.6mg/kg/day
In ACUTE ASTHMA, systemic CORTICOSTEROIDS reduce airway inflammation and speed recovery. PREDNISOLONE is commonly used (1–2 mg/kg/day in children, maximum 40–50 mg). DEXAMETHASONE is an alternative, especially in children, at about 0.6 mg/kg/day (usually a single dose, maximum about 16 mg), often given for 1–2 days. Its long action means fewer doses and less vomiting of tablets, improving adherence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.06mg/kg/day — 0.06 mg/kg is ten times TOO LOW — it would not be effective.
• 6mg/kg/day — 6 mg/kg is ten times TOO HIGH — a dangerous overdose.
• 60mg/kg/day — 60 mg/kg is a massive overdose.

⭐ Key point: Steroids take 4–6 hours to start working, so give them early, alongside bronchodilators (salbutamol, ipratropium) and oxygen.$x$, null),
(62, $x$A thoraco-abdominal incision on the right side gives access to a:$x$, $x$Lacerated liver$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Lacerated liver
A THORACO-ABDOMINAL incision crosses from the chest into the abdomen through the diaphragm. On the RIGHT side, it gives excellent exposure of the LIVER (especially the large right lobe and its posterior segments), the inferior vena cava, and the right side of the diaphragm. It is used for major LIVER LACERATIONS and complex liver resections, where a standard abdominal incision does not give enough access.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ruptured spleen — The SPLEEN is on the LEFT side — a right incision would not reach it.
• Oesophagogastric injury — Oesophagogastric injuries (lower oesophagus, stomach) are usually approached from the LEFT thoraco-abdominal route.
• None of the above — The liver is correctly accessed by this approach, so "none" is wrong.

⭐ Key point: Right lower rib fractures should raise suspicion of liver injury; look for signs of internal bleeding (tachycardia, hypotension, abdominal distension).$x$, null),
(63, $x$Type I respiratory failure is also known as:$x$, $x$Hypoxaemic failure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypoxaemic failure
TYPE I RESPIRATORY FAILURE is also called HYPOXAEMIC respiratory failure. It is defined by a LOW PaO2 (below 60 mmHg / 8 kPa) with a NORMAL or LOW PaCO2. It is a problem of GAS EXCHANGE/oxygenation (V/Q mismatch, shunt, diffusion problems), e.g. pneumonia, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, pulmonary embolism, asthma (early). The CO2 is normal or low because breathing effort is usually increased.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ventilatory failure — VENTILATORY failure is TYPE II (the "pump" fails to move enough air).
• Hypercapnic failure — HYPERCAPNIC failure (high CO2) is TYPE II.
• Septicaemic failure — "Septicaemic failure" is not a recognised classification of respiratory failure.

⭐ Key point: Treat Type I with oxygen, PEEP/CPAP and treatment of the cause; Type II needs ventilatory support (NIV or intubation).$x$, null),
(64, $x$Mr. Amir (RN in Cardiac ICU) obtained cardiac output of a patient having 100 kg weight and report to the head nurse that the cardiac output is 3.5 Liter per minute. To confirm, whether the cardiac output is normal or abnormal, a more accurate measurement used is:$x$, $x$Cardiac Index$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cardiac Index
CARDIAC OUTPUT (CO) depends on body size — a large person needs more blood flow. CARDIAC INDEX (CI) = CO ÷ BODY SURFACE AREA (BSA), which adjusts for body size. Normal CI is about 2.5–4.0 L/min/m². For a 100 kg patient, a CO of 3.5 L/min may give a low CI (about 1.5–1.6), indicating low output.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stroke volume — Stroke volume alone doesn't adjust for body size.
• Heart rate — Heart rate alone doesn't adjust for body size.
• PCWP — PCWP measures left heart filling, not output.

⭐ Key point: CI below 2.2 L/min/m² with signs of hypoperfusion suggests cardiogenic shock.$x$, null),
(65, $x$A patient comes with severe chest pain, radiating to jaw and shoulder. His ECG shows ST elevations in lead II, lead III and lead AVF. What does it show?$x$, $x$Inferior MI$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inferior MI
ST ELEVATION in leads II, III and aVF (the INFERIOR leads, which look at the bottom wall of the heart) indicates an INFERIOR MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION. It is usually caused by blockage of the RIGHT CORONARY ARTERY (in about 80–90% of people). Reciprocal ST depression may be seen in leads I and aVL. Inferior MI can be complicated by bradycardia and heart block (the RCA supplies the SA and AV nodes) and RIGHT VENTRICULAR infarction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Posterior MI — Posterior MI shows ST DEPRESSION with tall R waves in V1–V3 (and ST elevation in V7–V9).
• Anterior MI — Anterior MI shows ST elevation in V1–V4 (LAD artery).
• Septal MI — Septal MI shows ST elevation in V1–V2.

⭐ Key point: In inferior MI, record right-sided leads (V4R) — if RV infarction is present, avoid nitrates and give fluids for hypotension.$x$, null),
(66, $x$Immediately after PAC insertion, which action confirms correct catheter placement?$x$, $x$A chest X-ray$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A chest X-ray
After inserting a PULMONARY ARTERY CATHETER (PAC, Swan-Ganz), a CHEST X-RAY is taken to CONFIRM CORRECT PLACEMENT — the catheter tip should be in a main pulmonary artery branch (not too far out into a small artery, where it could cause infarction or rupture) — and to exclude complications of central venous access such as PNEUMOTHORAX, haemothorax or catheter knotting. The pressure waveforms seen during insertion (right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → wedge) also guide placement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Auscultation of bowel sounds — Bowel sounds have nothing to do with PAC position.
• Checking pedal pulses only — Pedal pulses alone do not confirm catheter position.
• Measuring oral temperature — Oral temperature does not confirm position (the PAC itself measures core temperature).

⭐ Key point: Continuously monitor the PA waveform — a permanently "wedged" trace means the catheter has migrated; deflate the balloon and pull back per protocol.$x$, null),
(67, $x$Primary (essential) hypertension accounts for approximately what percentage of hypertension cases?$x$, $x$85–95%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 85–95%
PRIMARY (ESSENTIAL) HYPERTENSION has no single identifiable cause; it results from a mix of genetic, lifestyle and environmental factors (age, family history, high salt intake, obesity, inactivity, alcohol, stress). It accounts for about 85–95% of all hypertension cases. SECONDARY hypertension (about 5–15%) is caused by an identifiable condition: kidney disease, renal artery stenosis, Conn's syndrome, Cushing's syndrome, phaeochromocytoma, thyroid disease, coarctation of the aorta, pregnancy, or drugs (e.g. steroids, oral contraceptives, NSAIDs).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5–15% — 5–15% is the proportion of SECONDARY hypertension.
• 50% — 50% underestimates primary hypertension.
• 100% — 100% ignores secondary causes.

⭐ Key point: Suspect secondary hypertension in young patients, sudden onset, very high or resistant BP, or low potassium.$x$, null),
(68, $x$Concerning the ECG, all of the following are incorrect EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Time and rate are measured on the horizontal axis and voltage on the vertical axis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Time and rate are measured on the horizontal axis and voltage on the vertical axis
On an ECG, TIME (and rate) is measured on the HORIZONTAL axis (each small square = 0.04 seconds at standard speed) and VOLTAGE (amplitude) on the VERTICAL axis (each small square = 0.1 mV). This statement is CORRECT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A waveform moving toward the top of the paper is a negative deflection — A waveform moving upwards is POSITIVE, not negative.
• A waveform moving toward the bottom of the paper is a positive deflection — A waveform moving downwards is NEGATIVE, not positive.
• The ECG waveform shows the heart's mechanical pumping force — The ECG shows ELECTRICAL activity, not mechanical pumping.

⭐ Key point: Pulseless electrical activity (PEA) shows that electrical activity can occur without effective pumping.$x$, null),
(69, $x$For an unconscious choking adult, the correct approach is to:$x$, $x$Start CPR, checking the mouth before each breath$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Start CPR, checking the mouth before each breath
For an UNCONSCIOUS CHOKING ADULT, call for help and START CPR immediately (30 compressions : 2 breaths). Chest compressions raise the pressure inside the chest and may dislodge the obstruction. BEFORE EACH ATTEMPT AT BREATHS, LOOK IN THE MOUTH and remove any VISIBLE object with your fingers (no blind finger sweeps). Continue until the object is cleared and the patient breathes, or help arrives.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait for the ambulance without doing anything — Waiting for the ambulance without action allows brain damage and death from hypoxia.
• Perform abdominal thrusts only — Abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre) are for CONSCIOUS choking adults; once unconscious, CPR is started.
• Place immediately in recovery position — The recovery position does nothing to clear a blocked airway.

⭐ Key point: For a CONSCIOUS choking adult: encourage coughing; if ineffective, give up to 5 back blows then up to 5 abdominal thrusts, repeating.$x$, null),
(70, $x$The amount of blood which the heart pumps in one cardiac contraction is called:$x$, $x$Stroke Volume$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stroke Volume
STROKE VOLUME (SV) is the amount of blood the LEFT VENTRICLE ejects with ONE contraction (heartbeat) — about 60–100 mL in an adult at rest. It depends on three factors: PRELOAD (how full the ventricle is before contraction), AFTERLOAD (the resistance it pumps against), and CONTRACTILITY (the strength of contraction). CARDIAC OUTPUT = stroke volume × heart rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pre-load — PRELOAD is the stretch/filling of the ventricle at the end of diastole.
• After Load — AFTERLOAD is the resistance the ventricle must overcome to eject blood.
• Cardiac Output — CARDIAC OUTPUT is the amount pumped per MINUTE, not per beat.

⭐ Key point: Stroke volume falls in hypovolaemia, heart failure and tamponade; the body compensates by increasing heart rate.$x$, null),
(71, $x$All are clinical features of pulmonary oedema EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Syncope$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Syncope
PULMONARY OEDEMA is fluid in the lung tissue and alveoli, usually from LEFT HEART FAILURE (high pressure in the lung capillaries pushes fluid out). Classic features: EXTREME DYSPNOEA (especially lying flat — orthopnoea — and waking breathless at night), COUGH with PINK, FROTHY SPUTUM (fluid mixed with air and a little blood), WHEEZING (fluid narrows the airways — "cardiac asthma"), crackles at both lung bases, sweating, anxiety, tachycardia and low SpO2. SYNCOPE (fainting) is NOT a typical feature of pulmonary oedema; it suggests arrhythmia, aortic stenosis, or other causes of sudden reduced brain blood flow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cough with pink, frothy sputum — Pink, frothy sputum IS a classic feature.
• Extreme dyspnoea — Extreme dyspnoea IS a classic feature.
• Inspiratory and expiratory wheezing — Wheezing IS a feature ("cardiac asthma").

⭐ Key point: Treatment: sit the patient upright, give oxygen/CPAP, IV furosemide and nitrates, and treat the cause (e.g. MI, hypertension, arrhythmia).$x$, null),
(72, $x$Myocardial oxygen demand is increased by the followings EXCEPT$x$, $x$Sleep$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Sleep
MYOCARDIAL OXYGEN DEMAND rises when the heart has to work harder: higher HEART RATE, greater CONTRACTILITY, and increased WALL TENSION (from high afterload or a thick or dilated ventricle). LEFT VENTRICULAR HYPERTROPHY (more muscle to supply, higher wall tension), FEVER (higher metabolic rate and heart rate) and HYPERTHYROIDISM (faster, stronger heartbeat) all INCREASE oxygen demand. SLEEP DECREASES it — heart rate and BP fall during sleep, reducing the heart's workload.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Left ventricular hypertrophy — LV hypertrophy increases demand.
• Fever — Fever increases demand.
• Hyperthyroidism — Hyperthyroidism increases demand.

⭐ Key point: In patients with ischaemic heart disease, reduce oxygen demand by controlling pain, fever, anxiety, tachycardia and hypertension, and by promoting rest and sleep.$x$, null),
(73, $x$Which of the following is NOT a hemodynamic effects and benefits of intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP);$x$, $x$Increase ventricular wall tension$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Increase ventricular wall tension
An INTRA-AORTIC BALLOON PUMP (IABP) is a balloon in the descending aorta that INFLATES during DIASTOLE (pushing blood back towards the coronary arteries → INCREASES CORONARY BLOOD FLOW) and DEFLATES just before SYSTOLE (creating a "vacuum" that lowers aortic pressure → REDUCES AFTERLOAD). Lower afterload means the left ventricle needs less pressure and less WALL TENSION to eject blood, so its OXYGEN DEMAND FALLS and CARDIAC OUTPUT INCREASES. "Increase ventricular wall tension" is the OPPOSITE of what the IABP does, so it is NOT a benefit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase cardiac output — Increased cardiac output IS a benefit.
• Decrease o2 demand — Decreased oxygen demand IS a benefit.
• Increase coronary flow — Increased coronary flow IS a benefit.

⭐ Key point: Nursing care: check limb perfusion on the insertion side, keep the leg straight, monitor timing, watch for bleeding and balloon rupture (blood in the tubing).$x$, null),
(74, $x$Afterload for the left ventricle is most directly represented by:$x$, $x$Systemic vascular resistance (SVR)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Systemic vascular resistance (SVR)
AFTERLOAD is the RESISTANCE the ventricle must overcome to eject blood. For the LEFT VENTRICLE, afterload is mainly determined by the resistance of the systemic arteries — the SYSTEMIC VASCULAR RESISTANCE (SVR) (plus aortic valve stenosis if present). High SVR (vasoconstriction, hypertension) increases afterload; low SVR (vasodilation, sepsis) reduces it. Normal SVR: about 800–1200 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) — PVR (pulmonary vascular resistance) represents RIGHT ventricular afterload.
• Central venous pressure — CVP reflects RIGHT ventricular PRELOAD.
• Stroke volume index — Stroke volume index reflects the heart's output, not afterload.

⭐ Key point: Afterload reduction (e.g. vasodilators, IABP) helps a failing heart pump more blood.$x$, null),
(75, $x$A condition characterised by a prolonged PR interval more than 0.2 secs in ECG is called:$x$, $x$First degree Av block$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: First degree Av block
FIRST-DEGREE AV BLOCK is defined by a PROLONGED PR INTERVAL of MORE THAN 0.20 SECONDS (more than 5 small squares on the ECG). Every P wave is still followed by a QRS (no dropped beats); conduction through the AV node is simply delayed. It is usually benign, often caused by drugs (beta-blockers, digoxin, calcium channel blockers), high vagal tone, or inferior MI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Second degree AV block — Second-degree AV block has some P waves NOT followed by a QRS.
• Mobiz Type II AV block — Mobitz type II is a form of second-degree block with dropped beats and a constant PR.
• Complete block — Complete (third-degree) block has NO relationship between P waves and QRS complexes.

⭐ Key point: Normal PR interval: 0.12–0.20 seconds (3–5 small squares).$x$, null),
(76, $x$On an ECG, the P wave reflects atrial depolarisation initiated by the:$x$, $x$SA node$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: SA node
The SINOATRIAL (SA) NODE, in the wall of the right atrium, is the heart's natural PACEMAKER. It generates electrical impulses (normally 60–100 per minute) that spread across both atria, causing ATRIAL DEPOLARISATION — seen as the P WAVE on the ECG — followed by atrial contraction. The impulse then travels to the AV node.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Left bundle branch — The left bundle branch conducts impulses through the ventricles (part of the QRS).
• AV node — The AV node delays the impulse between atria and ventricles (the PR segment).
• Bachmann's bundle — Bachmann's bundle carries the impulse from the right to left atrium, but does not INITIATE it.

⭐ Key point: In sinus rhythm, every QRS is preceded by an upright P wave in lead II.$x$, null),
(77, $x$Which mechanism helps maintain glomerular filtration pressure when renal perfusion falls, mediated by angiotensin II?$x$, $x$Constriction of the efferent arteriole$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Constriction of the efferent arteriole
When renal perfusion falls, the kidney releases RENIN, producing ANGIOTENSIN II. Angiotensin II CONSTRICTS the EFFERENT ARTERIOLE (the vessel leaving the glomerulus) more than the afferent. This "backs up" blood in the glomerulus, MAINTAINING GLOMERULAR FILTRATION PRESSURE and GFR despite low blood flow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dilation of the efferent arteriole — Dilating the efferent arteriole would LOWER filtration pressure (this is what ACE inhibitors do).
• Dilation of both arterioles equally — Dilating both arterioles equally would not maintain filtration pressure.
• Constriction of the afferent arteriole — Constricting the afferent arteriole would REDUCE blood entering the glomerulus and lower GFR.

⭐ Key point: ACE inhibitors and ARBs block this mechanism, so they can cause AKI in dehydration or renal artery stenosis.$x$, null),
(78, $x$At 10 years, survival on dialysis alone (without transplant) is approximately:$x$, $x$8.49%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 8.49%
Long-term survival on DIALYSIS ALONE (without a kidney transplant) is poor, because of cardiovascular disease, infections and other complications. Registry data show that only about 10% or fewer (around 8.5% in the source used) of patients are still alive after 10 YEARS on dialysis alone. Kidney transplantation gives much better long-term survival and quality of life.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 55.07% — 55% is far too high for 10-year survival on dialysis alone.
• 74.84% — 75% is far too high.
• 95% — 95% is far too high.

⭐ Key point: Survival depends on age, diabetes, heart disease and access to adequate dialysis.$x$, null),
(79, $x$Storage lower urinary tract symptoms include the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Hesitancy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hesitancy
LOWER URINARY TRACT SYMPTOMS (LUTS) are grouped into: STORAGE symptoms (problems holding urine): FREQUENCY, URGENCY, NOCTURIA (waking at night to pass urine), urge incontinence; and VOIDING symptoms (problems emptying): HESITANCY (delay in starting), weak stream, straining, intermittent stream, terminal dribbling and incomplete emptying. HESITANCY is a VOIDING symptom, not a storage symptom.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Frequency — Frequency IS a storage symptom.
• Urgency — Urgency IS a storage symptom.
• Nocturia — Nocturia IS a storage symptom.

⭐ Key point: Voiding symptoms are typical of benign prostatic hyperplasia; storage symptoms are typical of overactive bladder or UTI.$x$, null),
(80, $x$Approximately what percentage of resting cardiac output do the kidneys normally receive?$x$, $x$20-25%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 20-25%
The KIDNEYS receive about 20–25% of the RESTING CARDIAC OUTPUT — about 1–1.25 litres of blood per minute — even though they weigh only about 300 g (0.5% of body weight). This huge blood flow is not for the kidneys' own oxygen needs; it is needed to FILTER large volumes of plasma (about 180 litres per day) to remove waste products and regulate fluids, electrolytes and acid–base balance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40% — 40% is too high.
• 5% — 5% is too low.
• 10% — 10% is too low.

⭐ Key point: Because the kidneys depend on high blood flow, any hypotension or hypovolaemia quickly reduces GFR and can cause pre-renal AKI — maintain MAP above 65 mmHg.$x$, null),
(81, $x$Post-nephrectomy hospital stay typically ranges from:$x$, $x$2–7 days$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 2–7 days
NEPHRECTOMY is surgical removal of a kidney (for cancer, trauma, a non-functioning kidney, or living donation). Hospital stay usually ranges from about 2 TO 7 DAYS: about 2–3 days after LAPAROSCOPIC surgery, and about 5–7 days after OPEN surgery, depending on pain control, mobility, bowel function and complications. Nursing care focuses on monitoring for bleeding (BP, pulse, wound, drains, haemoglobin), urine output (function of the remaining kidney), pain control, chest physiotherapy (flank incisions near the diaphragm limit breathing), and early mobilisation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2–4 weeks — 2–4 weeks is unusually long and suggests complications.
• 6 months — 6 months is far too long.
• Discharge is always same-day — Same-day discharge is not standard after nephrectomy.

⭐ Key point: After nephrectomy, avoid nephrotoxic drugs (e.g. NSAIDs) to protect the remaining kidney.$x$, null),
(82, $x$A 68-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia presents with anuria and a palpably distended bladder. This is most consistent with which category of AKI?$x$, $x$Postrenal$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Postrenal
BENIGN PROSTATIC HYPERPLASIA (BPH) can BLOCK the outflow of urine from the bladder. ANURIA with a PALPABLY DISTENDED BLADDER shows the kidneys are making urine but it cannot drain out — this is POST-RENAL (obstructive) AKI. Treatment: urinary catheterisation (often suprapubic if urethral fails). Watch for post-obstructive diuresis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prerenal — Pre-renal AKI is due to reduced kidney blood flow.
• Intrarenal — Intrarenal AKI is due to kidney tissue damage.
• Functional — "Functional" is not a standard category here.

⭐ Key point: A bladder scan or palpation can quickly confirm retention.$x$, null),
(83, $x$An unresponsive patient shows no abnormal movements, and continuous EEG shows a normal background without epileptiform activity. This clinical picture is best described as what?$x$, $x$Unconsciousness/coma without seizure activity$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Unconsciousness/coma without seizure activity
An UNRESPONSIVE patient with NO ABNORMAL MOVEMENTS and a CONTINUOUS EEG showing a NORMAL BACKGROUND WITHOUT EPILEPTIFORM ACTIVITY is UNCONSCIOUS (COMA) WITHOUT SEIZURE ACTIVITY. The cause must be found (metabolic, toxic, structural).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An active tonic-clonic seizure — An active tonic-clonic seizure shows obvious movements and EEG seizure activity.
• Nonconvulsive status epilepticus — Non-convulsive status epilepticus shows seizure activity on EEG without obvious movements.
• A myoclonic seizure — A myoclonic seizure involves jerks.

⭐ Key point: EEG is important in unexplained coma to rule out non-convulsive seizures.$x$, null),
(84, $x$An unconscious patient's airway is being protected. The nurse should recognise that airway reflexes (gag/cough) are typically lost when the GCS falls to approximately:$x$, $x$8 or below$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 8 or below
When the GCS falls to 8 OR BELOW, protective airway reflexes (gag and cough) are typically lost or impaired. The patient cannot protect their airway from obstruction or aspiration. This is why "GCS 8 — intubate" is a common rule.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15 — GCS 15 is fully alert.
• 12 — GCS 12 usually maintains airway reflexes.
• 10, with no significance below this — Below 10 is significant, and reflexes are usually lost at 8 or below.

⭐ Key point: Position unconscious patients on their side and have suction ready until the airway is secured.$x$, null),
(85, $x$In a pregnant patient having eclamptic seizures, the first-line treatment is:$x$, $x$Magnesium sulphate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Magnesium sulphate
ECLAMPSIA is the occurrence of SEIZURES in a woman with pre-eclampsia. The FIRST-LINE treatment for eclamptic seizures — and for preventing further seizures — is MAGNESIUM SULPHATE: loading dose 4 g IV over 5–15 minutes, then a maintenance infusion of 1 g/hour (Zuspan regimen) or IM regimen (Pritchard). Large trials (Collaborative Eclampsia Trial, Magpie Trial) showed magnesium sulphate is MORE EFFECTIVE than diazepam and phenytoin at preventing recurrent seizures and REDUCES MATERNAL DEATH.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lorazepam — Lorazepam is first-line for most other seizures, but not for eclampsia.
• Phenytoin — Phenytoin is less effective than magnesium in eclampsia.
• Diazepam — Diazepam is less effective in eclampsia and causes more maternal and neonatal sedation.

⭐ Key point: During magnesium therapy, monitor reflexes, respiratory rate and urine output hourly; keep calcium gluconate (antidote) at the bedside. Delivery is the definitive treatment of eclampsia.$x$, null),
(86, $x$Which ICP monitoring device is considered the gold standard because it also allows therapeutic drainage of cerebrospinal fluid?$x$, $x$Intraventricular catheter (external ventricular drain)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intraventricular catheter (external ventricular drain)
The INTRAVENTRICULAR CATHETER (EXTERNAL VENTRICULAR DRAIN, EVD) is placed in a lateral ventricle. It is the GOLD STANDARD for ICP monitoring because it is accurate, can be recalibrated, AND allows THERAPEUTIC DRAINAGE of CSF to lower ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Subarachnoid bolt — Subarachnoid bolts are less accurate and can't drain CSF.
• Epidural sensor — Epidural sensors are less accurate.
• Intraparenchymal fiberoptic monitor — Intraparenchymal monitors are accurate but can't drain CSF.

⭐ Key point: EVDs carry a risk of infection (ventriculitis) — maintain strict asepsis.$x$, null),
(87, $x$All of the following are hyperkinetic involuntary movement disorders EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Bradykinesia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Bradykinesia
Movement disorders are divided into HYPERKINETIC (too much, unwanted movement) and HYPOKINETIC (too little movement). HYPERKINETIC disorders include: CHOREA (brief, irregular, dance-like jerks — e.g. Huntington's disease), ATHETOSIS (slow, writhing movements of the hands and feet — e.g. cerebral palsy, kernicterus), TORSION DYSTONIA (sustained twisting muscle contractions and abnormal postures), tremor, tics, myoclonus and ballismus. BRADYKINESIA (slowness of movement) is HYPOKINETIC — a key feature of PARKINSON'S DISEASE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chorea — Chorea IS hyperkinetic.
• Athetosis — Athetosis IS hyperkinetic.
• Torsion dystonia — Torsion dystonia IS hyperkinetic.

⭐ Key point: Parkinson's disease features: bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor and postural instability.$x$, null),
(88, $x$Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP) is calculated as:$x$, $x$Mean Arterial Pressure minus Intracranial Pressure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mean Arterial Pressure minus Intracranial Pressure
CEREBRAL PERFUSION PRESSURE (CPP) = MEAN ARTERIAL PRESSURE (MAP) − INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP). It represents the pressure driving blood into the brain. Target CPP is usually 60–70 mmHg in TBI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Systolic Blood Pressure plus Intracranial Pressure — Systolic plus ICP is incorrect.
• Heart Rate divided by Intracranial Pressure — Heart rate divided by ICP is incorrect.
• Diastolic Blood Pressure minus Heart Rate — Diastolic minus heart rate is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: Low CPP causes brain ischaemia; raise MAP or lower ICP.$x$, null),
(89, $x$What is the normal range for intracranial pressure (ICP) in adults?$x$, $x$5-15 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 5-15 mmHg
INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP) is the pressure inside the skull. In a resting adult lying down, NORMAL ICP is about 5–15 mmHg (7–20 cmH2O). ICP above 20–22 mmHg is considered RAISED and usually needs treatment, because high ICP reduces cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP − ICP) and can cause brain ischaemia and herniation. Coughing, straining and suctioning cause brief rises that settle quickly in a normal brain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0-5 mmHg — 0–5 mmHg is low; very low ICP can occur after over-drainage of CSF.
• 20-30 mmHg — 20–30 mmHg is RAISED.
• 30-40 mmHg — 30–40 mmHg is severely raised and dangerous.

⭐ Key point: Nursing measures to keep ICP down: head-up 30°, neck neutral, avoid tight tube ties, minimise suctioning, treat pain, fever and agitation, and avoid hypoxia and high CO2.$x$, null),
(90, $x$All of the following are levels of impaired consciousness EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Alertness$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Alertness
Consciousness ranges from fully ALERT (normal) through various levels of IMPAIRMENT: CONFUSION, DELIRIUM (acute fluctuating confusion with poor attention), lethargy/drowsiness, obtundation, STUPOR (responds only to strong, repeated stimuli) and COMA (cannot be roused, no purposeful response). ALERTNESS is the NORMAL state of full consciousness — awake, attentive, and responding appropriately — so it is NOT a level of impaired consciousness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stupor — Stupor IS a level of impaired consciousness.
• Delirium — Delirium IS a disturbance of consciousness.
• Coma — Coma IS the deepest level of impaired consciousness.

⭐ Key point: Describe consciousness objectively with the GCS (and AVPU in emergencies) rather than vague terms.$x$, null),
(91, $x$Unconsciousness include all the following EXCEPT$x$, $x$Awareness of self and environment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Awareness of self and environment
UNCONSCIOUSNESS is a state of abnormal cerebral activity and depressed brain function, where the person is UNRESPONSIVE to internal and external stimuli. AWARENESS of SELF and ENVIRONMENT is the definition of CONSCIOUSNESS — the opposite of unconsciousness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A state of abnormal cerebral activity — Abnormal cerebral activity IS part of unconsciousness.
• Unresponsive to internal and external environmental changes — Unresponsiveness IS part of unconsciousness.
• Depression of cerebral function — Depressed cerebral function IS part of unconsciousness.

⭐ Key point: Causes of unconsciousness: head injury, stroke, hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, drugs, infection, seizures.$x$, null),
(92, $x$A nurse is performing hygiene care for an unconscious patient. To prevent complications, the nurse should:$x$, $x$Ensure the patient's head is elevated to at least 30 degrees during care$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ensure the patient's head is elevated to at least 30 degrees during care
Unconscious patients are at risk of ASPIRATION during care (e.g. mouth care, bathing). ELEVATING THE HEAD to at least 30 degrees (or turning the patient to the side) reduces the risk of aspiration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bathe the patient with cold water to stimulate a response — Cold water bathing causes hypothermia.
• Use a cotton swab to clean the patient's ears — Cotton swabs can damage the ear canal and eardrum.
• Use a firm bristle toothbrush to clean the teeth — Firm bristle brushes can damage gums; use a soft brush.

⭐ Key point: Use suction during mouth care and check the airway.$x$, null),
(93, $x$Therapeutic hyperventilation used briefly as a temporizing measure for acute herniation should target a PaCO2 of approximately what value?$x$, $x$30-35 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 30-35 mmHg
In an EMERGENCY of acute BRAIN HERNIATION (e.g. a blown pupil, Cushing's response), brief HYPERVENTILATION lowers PaCO2. Low CO2 makes the brain's blood vessels CONSTRICT, reducing cerebral blood volume and quickly lowering ICP. The target is a PaCO2 of about 30–35 mmHg. This is only a short-term "bridge" while giving osmotherapy (mannitol or hypertonic saline) and arranging CT and neurosurgery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15-20 mmHg — 15–20 mmHg causes severe vasoconstriction, cutting off blood supply and causing brain ischaemia.
• 45-50 mmHg — 45–50 mmHg (high CO2) causes vasodilation and RAISES ICP.
• 55-60 mmHg — 55–60 mmHg would raise ICP even more.

⭐ Key point: Avoid prolonged or routine hyperventilation in brain injury — it can worsen ischaemia. Normal target PaCO2 is 35–45 mmHg.$x$, null),
(94, $x$A CSF leak is suspected from a patient's nose after head trauma. Which bedside test helps confirm CSF rather than mucus?$x$, $x$Halo (ring) sign on gauze$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Halo (ring) sign on gauze
After head trauma, clear fluid from the nose (rhinorrhoea) or ear (otorrhoea) may be CSF leaking through a skull base fracture. The HALO (RING) SIGN is a simple bedside test: a drop of the fluid is placed on gauze or filter paper. If CSF is present (mixed with blood), it separates into a central red/pink spot surrounded by a clear or yellowish "halo" ring, because CSF spreads further than blood. Confirmation is by testing for GLUCOSE (CSF contains glucose; mucus does not) or, more accurately, BETA-2 TRANSFERRIN.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Kernig's sign — Kernig's sign tests for meningeal irritation.
• Doll's eye test — The doll's eye test (oculocephalic reflex) assesses brainstem function.
• Babinski test — The Babinski test assesses upper motor neurone damage.

⭐ Key point: With a suspected CSF leak: avoid nasal tubes and nasal suction, tell the patient not to blow the nose, keep head elevated, and watch for meningitis.$x$, null),
(95, $x$What does the brainstem control?$x$, $x$Breathing$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Breathing
The BRAINSTEM (midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata) connects the brain to the spinal cord and controls VITAL, automatic functions: BREATHING (respiratory centres in the medulla and pons), HEART RATE and BLOOD PRESSURE (cardiovascular centres), CONSCIOUSNESS and the sleep–wake cycle (reticular activating system), swallowing, coughing and vomiting reflexes, and most CRANIAL NERVE functions (pupil reactions, eye movements, facial sensation, gag reflex).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Feelings — Feelings and emotions are processed mainly by the limbic system and frontal lobes.
• Balance — Balance and coordination are mainly controlled by the cerebellum.
• Emotions — Emotions are processed by the limbic system (e.g. amygdala).

⭐ Key point: Brainstem death means permanent loss of brainstem reflexes and the ability to breathe; it is legally recognised as death.$x$, null),
(96, $x$Mr kifakat is on mechanical ventilator in ICU with a diagnosis of Brain death, what is his GCS$x$, $x$3/15$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 3/15
In BRAIN DEATH, there is NO brain function at all. On the GLASGOW COMA SCALE: EYES — no eye opening = 1; VERBAL — no verbal response = 1 (or "T" if intubated); MOTOR — no motor response = 1. The total is the LOWEST possible score: 3/15. (Spinal reflexes may still be present, because the spinal cord can work independently.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6/15 — 6/15 means some responses are present.
• 4/15 — 4/15 means at least one response (e.g. extension or eye opening to pain).
• 5/15 — 5/15 means some responses are present.

⭐ Key point: Brain death is confirmed by formal brainstem testing by two senior doctors (after excluding drugs, hypothermia and metabolic causes), not by GCS alone.$x$, null),
(97, $x$Alcohol withdrawal symptoms typically begin how long after the last drink?$x$, $x$6–24 hours$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 6–24 hours
In people dependent on alcohol, WITHDRAWAL symptoms usually begin about 6–24 HOURS after the LAST DRINK, as blood alcohol falls: tremor ("the shakes"), anxiety, sweating, nausea, vomiting, tachycardia, hypertension and insomnia. WITHDRAWAL SEIZURES may occur at 12–48 hours. DELIRIUM TREMENS (confusion, hallucinations, fever, severe autonomic instability) usually appears at 48–72 hours and can be fatal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediately, within minutes — Symptoms do not begin immediately (within minutes).
• 1–2 weeks — 1–2 weeks is too late for the onset of withdrawal.
• Only after 1 month — 1 month is far too late.

⭐ Key point: Ask about alcohol intake on admission, use a withdrawal scale (CIWA-Ar), give benzodiazepines as needed, and give IV thiamine before glucose.$x$, null),
(98, $x$A patient in the ICU is agitated, disoriented, and pulling at IV lines. The nurse FIRST:$x$, $x$Assesses for pain or urinary retention$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assesses for pain or urinary retention
An AGITATED, DISORIENTED ICU patient pulling at lines may be in DELIRIUM, but the agitation often has a TREATABLE CAUSE: PAIN, a FULL BLADDER (URINARY RETENTION — e.g. blocked catheter), hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, sepsis, drug or alcohol withdrawal, or discomfort (positioning, thirst). The FIRST action is to ASSESS for these causes — e.g. use a pain scale (CPOT/BPS), check SpO2 and glucose, check the catheter and bladder (bladder scan), while keeping the patient safe.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administers haloperidol — Haloperidol may be considered later for distressing agitation, but not before looking for the cause.
• Applies restraints — Restraints are a last resort; they can increase agitation and injury.
• Increases sedation — Increasing sedation may mask the cause and worsen delirium.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause, reorient the patient, involve family, and use the least restrictive measures.$x$, null),
(99, $x$A patient with alcohol withdrawal delirium should receive:$x$, $x$Benzodiazepines$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Benzodiazepines
ALCOHOL WITHDRAWAL DELIRIUM (DELIRIUM TREMENS) is caused by sudden loss of alcohol's calming effect on GABA receptors, causing over-excitation of the brain. BENZODIAZEPINES (e.g. diazepam, lorazepam, chlordiazepoxide) act on the SAME GABA receptors as alcohol, so they calm the brain, control agitation and tremor, and PREVENT SEIZURES. They are the first-line treatment. IV THIAMINE should also be given to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy, along with fluids and electrolyte correction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Antipsychotics — Antipsychotics (e.g. haloperidol) may lower the seizure threshold; they are only add-ons for severe hallucinations.
• Opioids — Opioids do not treat alcohol withdrawal.
• Barbiturates — Barbiturates (e.g. phenobarbital) may be used for benzodiazepine-resistant cases, but are not first-line.

⭐ Key point: Monitor sedation and breathing closely during high-dose benzodiazepine treatment; severe cases need ICU.$x$, null),
(100, $x$A patient with hypoactive delirium is at risk for:$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
HYPOACTIVE DELIRIUM is the "quiet" type: the patient is drowsy, withdrawn, slow to respond and inactive. Because they are not disruptive, it is often MISSED. These patients are at risk of: FALLS (confusion and weakness when they try to get up), MALNUTRITION and dehydration (they eat and drink poorly), PRESSURE ULCERS (they lie still for long periods), aspiration, DVT and longer hospital stays. So "ALL OF THE ABOVE" is correct. Hypoactive delirium also carries a higher mortality than the hyperactive type.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Falls — Falls are only one risk.
• Malnutrition — Malnutrition is only one risk.
• Pressure ulcers — Pressure ulcers are only one risk.

⭐ Key point: Screen every patient regularly with CAM-ICU or 4AT to detect hypoactive delirium.$x$, null)
),
upd as (
  update public.questions qq
     set exp = r.exp,
         ans_idx = coalesce((select (t.i - 1)::int from jsonb_array_elements_text(qq.opts) with ordinality t(v, i) where t.v = r.fixed_answer), qq.ans_idx)
    from r
   where qq.q = r.q
     and qq.opts ->> qq.ans_idx = r.answer
  returning r.n
)
select count(distinct n) as "questions updated (should be 100)", count(*) as "rows changed" from upd;
